  "source_example": {
    "text": "Stockholm ran congestion charges as a full-scale trial from 3 January to 31 July 2006. After a referendum in September 2006, in which a majority of city voters said yes, the charges became permanent on 1 August 2007. The charge was collected at 18 control points on the roads into and out of the inner city. Cameras read each car's number plate, so drivers did not have to stop. The price depended on the time of day. A passage in the busiest half hours cost 20 Swedish kronor (SEK), the shoulder periods around them cost 15 SEK, and the remaining daytime periods cost 10 SEK. No charge applied in the evening, at night, at weekends or on public holidays. A driver paid at most 60 SEK in one day, however often they crossed the cordon. Traffic across the cordon fell by about 20 percent compared with the year before, and queues on the main approach roads became much shorter. Planners explained the result simply: drivers who could move their trip to a cheaper half hour, or to public transport, did so. The through route Essingeleden was exempt at first, because it was the main bypass around the centre. Residents of the island of Lidingö also had an exemption at the start, since their only road link to the mainland ran through the charged zone. Public support was low before the trial and rose after people saw the shorter queues. The revenue was earmarked for transport investment in the Stockholm region. Later evaluations found that the traffic reduction lasted for years after the charges became permanent. The charges were raised in 2016, and Essingeleden began to be charged at the same time. Researchers have used the case to argue that a price which follows the daily peak can manage scarce road space better than a flat price. Critics pointed to the cost of running the camera system and asked whether a simpler flat fee would have worked. The Stockholm design is a three-step price ladder in which the top price is double the bottom one. It also shows that a daily cap protects people who must cross the cordon many times.",
    "length": "long",
